   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to establish the digital currency task force; and providing for the\nrepeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S9013",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9013-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Financial Services",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S9013\n\nSPONSOR: PHILLIPS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to establish the digital currency task force;\nand providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nEstablishes a commission to study how to regulate cryptocurrencies in\nNew York State\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 establishes the New York State digital currency task force.\n\nSection 2 states who the task force shall be comprised of. Section 3\nlists the responsibilities and roles of the taskforce. Section 4 is the\neffective date and the repeal date of this act.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIt is arguable that New York remains as the global financial capital\ncenter. We must make sure that we help foster an environment to lead the\nworld in the finance sector. Today, the New York Stock Exchange and\nNASDAQ are two of the most valuable stock exchanges in the world.  In\nNew York State, the financial sector makes up over eighteen percent of\nour tax revenue and is the state's premiere economic engine.\n\nWe, however, are not guaranteed to remain the world financial leader.\nIn fact in this economic environment, we are competing for financial\ninvestments and our standing the fiscal leader with London, Japan, Shan-\nghai and Hong Kong. In this digital and technological environment,\nhowever, New York is competing with virtually every global market and\ncur states for future financial transactions and activities. California,\nNevada and Delaware are gaining penetration for cryptocurrency invest-\nments and transactions.\n\nCryptocurrencies are making their way into the New York state digital\neconomy. A growing number of merchants accept cryptocurrency as payment\nincluding Microsoft, Expedia.com, Dell, Overstock.com, Reddit, Subway\nand Bloomberg.com. Companies and investors are raising capital and fund-\ning through Initial Coin Offerings (ICO).  Additionally, there are cryp-\ntocurrency ATMs across the state where New Yorkers are purchasing denom-\ninations of cryptocurrencies at conveniences stores and public places.\n\nAccording to Wikipedia, \"a cryptocurrency is a digital asset designed to\nwork as a medium of exchange using cryptography to secure the trans-\nactions and to control the creation of additional units of the curren-\ncy... Bitcoin, created in 2009, was the first decentralized cryptocur-\nrency.\" The virtue and danger of cryptocurrency is its anonymity and\ndecentralized model. As a state we must protect anti-money laundering,\nknow-your-customer laws and consumer and investor protections.\n\nWe must fully understand the implications of blockchain technology, its\nimpact on innovation, competition and regulation. New York must be a\nstate where: (1) consumers and investors are protected, (2) we prevent\nmoney laundering and (3) we know the customer to protect from nefarious\nuses and actors. At the same time, New York must lead the world in\nlegitimate digital and cryptocurrency investments and transactions.\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed repealed on\nDecember 15, 2020.\n",
